om/users/zzzeek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-10-07T21:42:25Z","updated_at":"2021-10-07T21:42:25Z","body":"> **Expected behavior** Warning (Error?) when trying to create a foreign key on a field with unique index with no unique constraint and no migration file generated at all, since there's no changes to the schema\r\n\r\nwhy?  is that not valid in SQL Server?  is SQL Server silently ignoring the constraint?   I'm not familiar with this behavior.   a \"unique index\" in a DB such as Postgresql is suitable as a target for an FK, and if you try to create an invalid FK, you'd get an error.\r\n\r\n","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/938176618/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"zzzeek","id":128223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyODIyMw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek","html_url":"https://github.com/zzzeek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/938582503","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/7160#issuecomment-938582503","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/7160","id":938582503,"node_id":"IC_kwDOCX5JB8438aHn","user":{"login":"dabajabaza","id":11490311,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExNDkwMzEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11490311?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza","html_url":"https://github.com/dabajabaza","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dabajabaza/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-10-08T11:55:39Z","updated_at":"2021-10-08T11:55:39Z","body":"@zzzeek MSSQL allows you to create a FK on a field with a unique index with no unique constraint. I think this is more a matter of semantics. Usually, when a developer wants to create a FK, he needs a unique constraint. And a unique index is needed rather for sorting, searching, filtering operations. I think explicit better than implicit. But I am not a very experienced db 